---
title: dinyd · creating and updating relationships
config:
  look: classic
  theme: dark
  themeVariables:
    actorBkg: "#333333"
    labelBoxBkgColor: "#fcd34d"
    labelTextColor: "#000000"
    noteTextColor: "#e0e0e0"
  themeCSS: |
    /* database · db */
    g.actor[transform] path { fill: #1c2f4a; stroke: #93c5fd; }
    /* database label clears the cylinder */
    g.actor[transform] + text { transform: translateY(12px); }
    g.actor[transform] + text tspan { paint-order: stroke; stroke: #1f1f1f; stroke-width: 6px; }
    /* collections · file */
    rect.actor:not([rx]) { fill: #3d3415; stroke: #fcd34d; }
  sequence:
    actorMargin: 80
    width: 200
    wrap: true
---
%% Behavior authority: this diagram and anchors.mermaid.
%% apply <doc> --line N --selector S [--bytes] [--ref] [--index | --worktree | --rev commit]: creates one relationship and writes its mode and first digest. In md it appends an inline HTML comment to the end of line N; in Mermaid it inserts a %% line after line N.
%% apply refuses when check's parser would not find the anchor at the written position, or when an anchor for S is already at the write position (line N in md, the line after N in Mermaid). --ref writes a dinyd-ref without a digest and cannot be combined with --bytes.
%% Without --bytes it uses the format's default mode (s1 when structural normalization exists, otherwise b1); a document unit always takes d1, and --bytes with a unit selector is a usage error.
%% update <doc> --line N --expect-selector S [--index | --worktree | --rev commit]: recomputes the digest with the recorded mode and writes it. A hand-written anchor without a digest is filled with the format's default mode. To change the mode, edit it in the anchor by hand and run update.
%% N is a 1-based line number counting LF only. update finds the anchor with check's parser and reads the selector from the current anchor in the working-tree document. The target content is read from the chosen basis.
%% update writes only when line N holds exactly one digest anchor matching S and the target resolves. Delegations are not update targets.
%% When apply · update cannot write, they write nothing and report an error; apply also names the basis it used. To judge with --index after writing, stage the document.
%% Writing changes only the anchor's slot and preserves every other byte and CRLF. There is no bulk update. If the computed digest is the same, nothing changes.
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  actor Worker as worker<br/>person · agent
  participant Doc@{ "type": "collections", "alias": "document" }
  participant Dinyd as dinyd

  Note over Worker,Dinyd: creating a relationship
  Worker->>Doc: open the document and judge the claim and its evidence
  Worker->>Dinyd: apply B.md --line N --selector A.cs#35;Foo
  Dinyd->>Doc: write the anchor and first digest

  Note over Worker,Dinyd: the evidence code changes
  Worker->>Dinyd: check
  Dinyd-->>Worker: B.md → A.cs#35;Foo: stale<br/>no line number · no document content

  rect rgb(23, 61, 37)
    Note over Worker,Doc: em · the span dinyd induces
    Worker->>Doc: open the document, find the anchor, and re-judge the claim
    opt the claim is wrong
      Worker->>Doc: fix the claim
    end
  end

  Worker->>Dinyd: update B.md --line N --expect-selector A.cs#35;Foo
  Dinyd->>Doc: rewrite only the digest
